\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b\fs24 \cf0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Echo Point, at civil twilight.
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 They begin on the lip of the escarpment with the Jamison Valley still largely in shadow. Immediately below and to the east are the Three Sisters, not remote postcard objects but enormous sandstone towers dropping into the valley. Across the basin, 
\f0\b Mount Solitary
\f1\b0  reads as a long, flat-backed dark mass rather than a peak; farther west the long wall of 
\f0\b Narrow Neck
\f1\b0  encloses the valley. At 05:15 the plateau sky would be whitening before much light reaches the valley floor, so TIGER starts by looking down into darkness while the upper sandstone catches the first usable light.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Three Sisters track into the Giant Stairway.
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 The route first brings them unnervingly close to the Three Sisters themselves. Then the Giant Stairway drops roughly 300 metres down the cliff face in almost a thousand steps: sections of rock-cut stair, steel stairs and railings fixed hard against the sandstone. The cliff is not scenery beside them; it is immediately at shoulder distance, orange-brown and overhanging in places. Through the outside railing there are abrupt views straight down onto treetops far below. On bends in the stair they can look across the open Jamison Valley to Mount Solitary, then lose the entire view when the staircase folds back against the cliff. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/things-to-do/walking-tracks/dardanelles-pass-loop-walking-track?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 National Parks NSW}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Bottom of Giant Stairway / Dardanelles Pass.
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 The change at the bottom is abrupt. They come off exposed cliff and steel into 
\f0\b deep, damp forest beneath the escarpment
\f1\b0 . Dardanelles Pass runs comparatively level near the upper talus beneath the cliffs, rather than dropping onto the open valley floor. There are huge fallen sandstone blocks, ferny gullies and a canopy of coachwood, sassafras and lilly pilly. The sandstone wall repeatedly appears through the trees on their left, sometimes as a vertical face, sometimes as dark overhangs stained by water. The Three Sisters are now seen 
\f0\b from underneath and behind
\f1\b0 , through foliage\'97a much stranger perspective than from Echo Point. Dardanelles is a maintained, legible track; TIGER is not yet hacking through scrub. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/education/~/~/link.aspx?_id=9BE24D12CEEF499DA7132811887A266A&_z=z&utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 National Parks NSW}}\
\ls2\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Leura Forest.
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 This is a very specific place rather than just \'93rainforest\'94. The track opens into an old recreation clearing beneath tall coachwoods and lilly pillies, with 
\f0\b large mossed sandstone boulders
\f1\b0 , a shelter/rotunda and the remains of the old Lady Carrington dining area. The canopy is high enough to give the place the feel of a dim outdoor hall. Even after sunrise it remains deeply shaded because it sits below the eastern cliffs. For TIGER, loaded with rifles and ammunition, the incongruity is useful: they briefly pass through somewhere designed for picnics before leaving the tourist network. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wildwalks.com.au/bushwalking-and-hiking-in-nsw/blue-mountains-leura/federal-pass-walking-track.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Wildwalks}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Leura Forest 
\f2 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  Marguerite Cascades 
\f2 \uc0\u8594 
\f0  Linda Falls.
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 From Leura Forest they turn 
\f0\b up Linda Creek
\f1\b0 , reversing part of the Federal Pass. The terrain begins closing around them again. They cross the creek on a footbridge and climb through a fern-filled gully, with timber and metal steps appearing among mossy sandstone. 
\f0\b Marguerite Cascades
\f1\b0  are not a giant Blue Mountains drop: the creek comes down in a succession of smaller falls and chutes into shallow pools beneath thick vegetation. Above that, 
\f0\b Linda Falls
\f1\b0  is a cleaner, slender fall in a narrow, heavily shaded gully. This section is wet, enclosed and green; water, tree-ferns, coachwood trunks and blackened sandstone dominate, with very little valley panorama. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wildwalks.com.au/bushwalking-and-hiking-in-nsw/blue-mountains-leura/federal-pass-walking-track.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Wildwalks}}\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The start of Lindeman Pass, just below Linda Falls.
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 Here the character of the route changes. The formed tourist track continues elsewhere; TIGER takes the faint historic route east. Lindeman Pass is cut along a 
\f0\b shale bench directly beneath the perpendicular sandstone cliffs and above the talus slope
\f1\b0 . So instead of being buried on the valley floor, they now travel along the seam where cliff meets slope. The cliff rises immediately on one side; the land falls away through forest on the other. When the trees open, 
\f0\b Mount Solitary suddenly fills the opposite side of the Jamison Valley
\f1\b0 , with Narrow Neck stretching away behind and west of them. This alternation\'97claustrophobic cliff, then a sudden huge view\'97is characteristic of this part of Lindeman. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hikingtheworld.blog/2023/04/14/lindeman-pass-copeland-pass/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Hiking the World}}\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	7	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Leura Falls.
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 This is their first substantial obstacle on Lindeman. The track runs towards the bottom of the major lower drop of 
\f0\b Leura Falls
\f1\b0 , where Leura Falls Creek comes over the escarpment into an enclosed, steep-sided amphitheatre. The route effectively loses its certainty around the water: they descend towards the creek, cross slippery rock below a smaller cascade, then regain the cliff line on the other side. The fall above gives them wet, dark sandstone and spray; looking out from the gully gives them bright Jamison Valley beyond. In the modern landscape there is also old sewer infrastructure, including a concrete inspection chamber, but whether any of that survives to 3012 is a separate world-building decision. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hikingtheworld.blog/2023/04/14/lindeman-pass-copeland-pass/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Hiking the World}}\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	8	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Lindeman ledges east of Leura Falls.
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 Past Leura Falls, they are properly on the forgotten pass. The route works along narrow shelves under the cliffs, interrupted by fallen trees, minor landslips and little watercourses coming straight over the sandstone above. In open sections, the valley drops away below the footpad and gives long views toward 
\f0\b Mount Solitary
\f1\b0 . In other sections the cliff presses close and the path passes beneath overhangs. Parts of the old route were supported by dry-stone retaining walls; even if you choose not to preserve those into 3012, the physical reason for them remains\'97the track is often literally a narrow bench built into steep ground. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fatcanyoners.org/2010/08/lindemanpass/?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 fat canyoners}}\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	9	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Microstrobos Falls.
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 The route goes directly across the base of this smaller waterfall. It is a narrow, fern-heavy gully where water drops down the cliff beside or over the route. This is not a distant viewpoint: TIGER goes 
\f0\b through the waterfall environment
\f1\b0 , over wet stones and beneath dense vegetation. It is one of several places where a heavily loaded operative would care much more about footing than scenery. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hikingtheworld.blog/2023/04/14/lindeman-pass-copeland-pass/"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 Hiking the World}}\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	10	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Gordon Falls \'97 specifically behind the lower fall.
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 This is visually distinctive enough to earn narrative space. Lindeman Pass runs 
\f0\b behind Gordon Falls
\f1\b0 , along a ledge tucked under the cliff. The water drops in front of the walkers, so they look outward through a falling curtain into the green gully and Jamison Valley. Behind them is dark sandstone; around their boots, wet rock, fern and moss; in front, moving water and daylight. The ledge itself can be surprisingly sheltered even while the fall is immediately outside it. The amount of water depends on recent weather, but the geometry does not. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://allthegearbutnoidea.blogspot.com/2021/03/wentworth-falls-to-katoomba-via.html?utm_source=chatgpt.com"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \ul \ulc3 \strokec3 All The Gear But No Idea}}\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 \
